Design a `_LedgerSpan` class: `body` (entry lines), and line range for stripping: `startLine`, `endLine` (inclusive).

Fenced span: opening fence line index → closing fence line index (or EOF if unterminated).
Unfenced span: heading line index → last ledger-entry line index.

Both parse via the same entry grammar. The fenced block body = lines between fences. The unfenced body = lines after the heading.

parse precedence: currently "last fenced block". New rule: candidate spans collected in order; try parse from LAST to FIRST, return first that yields items? Simpler: gather all candidate spans (fenced and unfenced), iterate from last to first, parse each, return first non-null. My test: unfenced first, fenced later → fenced is last → wins. If only unfenced → it parses.

Careful with the fenced parser: existing behavior takes the last fenced block even if it has no items → returns null (doesn't fall back to earlier blocks). E.g. test 'takes the LAST block when the message carries several' — last fenced has an item. And 'an empty or requirement-less block parses to null' — single empty block → null. Would my fallback change that case? With one empty fenced span: candidates=[fenced], parse → null items → continue to earlier spans — none → return null. Same. But if a text has an empty fenced block AFTER a good one... old behavior: parses LAST block (empty) → null. New: last fails → falls back to earlier good one → returns it. Slight behavior change in a weird corner; acceptable? Hmm, "takes the LAST block when the message carries several" pins the intent that last wins. To preserve exactly: parse only the LAST candidate span; if it has no items → null. But then an unfenced good ledger after which a stray empty fenced block appears would break. Corner of a corner. I'll go: consider candidates in reverse order, return the first that parses to a non-null ledger; if none parses, null. For the 'empty block parses to null' test: only candidate → null. For 'takes the LAST block': last parses → wins. Both still pass. OK.

Unfenced heading regex: `^\s{0,3}(?:#{1,6}\s+|\*\s*)task-ledger\s*:?\s*(?:\*\*)?\s*$` — hmm getting complicated. Simpler: strip markdown decoration: a line whose text, after removing leading `#`s, whitespace, and surrounding `**`, equals 'task-ledger' (case-insensitive, optional trailing colon). Implement:

```dart
bool _isLedgerHeading(String line) {
  var t = line.trim();
  if (t.startsWith('#')) {
    t = t.replaceFirst(RegExp(r'^#{1,6}\s*'), '');
  }
  t = t.trim();
  if (t.startsWith('**') && t.endsWith('**') && t.length > 4) {
    t = t.substring(2, t.length - 2).trim();
  }
  // Guard against prose: a heading is short and equals the token.
  return t.toLowerCase() == 'task-ledger' || t.toLowerCase() == 'task ledger';
}
```

Hmm 'task ledger' without hyphen — the unfenced dump in the bug had heading "task-ledger"; keep just 'task-ledger' plus optional trailing ':'. Also require the line to look like a heading: either starts with '#' or is bold-wrapped `**...**` or ends with ':'? My test has `## task-ledger` and `**task-ledger**`. For plain `task-ledger` alone on a line — allow too (heading-ish). The prose guard test: 'the task-ledger record is persisted hidden' — not alone on a line → rejected. Good.

Entry grammar for unfenced: same as fenced: `- key: value` starts entry; indented `key: value` continues. But in unfenced markdown, bullets could be ambiguous with the checklist the model also emits ("Checklist (требования и верификация)" section with `- [x] ...` items). We only start consuming at a `task-ledger` heading, and we stop at the first line that isn't blank / entry / continuation. Also require ≥1 requirement-bearing entry to accept the span (else not a ledger → don't strip).

Unfenced span extent: from heading line, consume subsequent lines while:
- blank line: tentatively include (may be separator between bullets? no — bullets are consecutive) — I'll consume blanks only BETWEEN entry lines? Simpler: iterate; a blank line: peek — if followed by more entry/continuation lines, skip it; else end span before it.
